INT. COURTHOUSE - BASEMENT HOLDING OFFICE - DAY

Cinder-block walls, a steel table, a ringing phone. A water-stained ceiling. One window, barred, at ankle level.

CORNELIA BASSETT, 40s, a defense attorney in a charcoal suit, paces with a stack of folders under one arm and a cold coffee in the other hand. She checks her watch. Again.

The intercom on the wall crackles.

HAROLD (V.O.)
(through the speaker)
Ms. Bassett? Ms. Bassett, are you there?

CORNELIA
I'm here, Harold.

HAROLD (V.O.)
They moved the hearing up.

CORNELIA
To when?

HAROLD (V.O.)
Ten minutes.

She sets down the coffee. It sloshes over the lip. She looks at the folders, at the door, at the clock.

CORNELIA
Where's my client?

HAROLD (V.O.)
Still upstairs. Processing.

CORNELIA
Tell them to hurry.

HAROLD (V.O.)
I did.

CORNELIA
Tell them again.

HAROLD (V.O.)
I'm coming down.

The intercom clicks off. She dumps the folders onto the table and tears through them, sheets flying. A door bangs somewhere above. Footsteps hammer the stairwell, and HAROLD VANTERPOOL, 60s, a sweating court clerk with a ring of keys and a bow tie askew, bursts in, holding a paper above his head like a prize. He wheezes and she whirls to meet him, snatching the paper, and he doubles over, hands on his knees, while she scans the page and slowly, grimly, begins to smile.

CORNELIA
This is the affidavit.

HAROLD
Found it in Judge Mercer's chambers.

CORNELIA
You stole it.

HAROLD
I borrowed it. Without asking.

CORNELIA
Harold, you beautiful man.

HAROLD
(catching his breath)
Ten minutes, Counselor.

CORNELIA
Eight, now.

She grabs her folders, squares her shoulders, and strides to the door. Harold straightens his bow tie.

HAROLD
Good luck.

CORNELIA
Luck is for the prosecution.
